    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Before-and-after photographs are useful, but they can create one misunderstanding: that the same procedure should produce the same result in everyone."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Hair transplantation is highly individual."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The visual result depends on donor hair available, size and location of hair loss, hair characteristics, surgical planning, healing and what happens to non-transplanted hair over time."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Donor density"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Someone with a strong donor area has more flexibility than someone whose donor hair is sparse or already affected by thinning."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Hair calibre"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Thicker hairs usually create more visual coverage per follicle than very fine hairs."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Curl and wave"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Hair texture influences how scalp visibility is perceived."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Colour contrast"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "If hair colour contrasts strongly with the scalp, spaces between hairs may be easier to notice."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Size of the treatment area"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A smaller frontal area may achieve stronger visual density than a large frontal, mid-scalp and crown restoration using the same donor supply."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Hairline and graft distribution"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Strategic placement matters. Where grafts are concentrated and how the hairline is designed can change visual impact substantially."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Existing native hair"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A person with miniaturised but still-present hair may look different from someone with a completely bald recipient area. Future loss of that native hair can also change appearance years later."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Healing and growth"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Biological growth varies. Transplanted hair often goes through an early shedding phase before gradual regrowth. The pace of visible growth and maturation differs between individuals."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Continued hair loss"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A successful transplant does not freeze the rest of the scalp. Non-transplanted follicles can continue thinning if the underlying pattern progresses."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Why results cannot be guaranteed from photographs"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A photograph does not show donor density, hair calibre, scalp examination, progression, health history or previous treatment."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A personalised assessment is therefore necessary before making meaningful predictions."
      }
    ],
